**`parseNote(from:)` function** (lines 26–36):
```swift
public func parseNote(from text: String) throws -> Note {
    let parts = text.split(separator: "\n---\n")
    guard parts.count == 4 else {
        throw NoteParseError.malformedFormat
    }
    let id = String(parts[0])
    let title = String(parts[1])
    let pinned = String(parts[2]) == "1"
    let body = String(parts[3])
    return Note(id: id, title: title, pinned: pinned, body: body, tags: extractTags(from: body))
}
```

Behavior:
- Splits on the literal `\n---\n` separator.
- If the result is not exactly 4 parts, throws `NoteParseError.malformedFormat`.
- Extracts `id`, `title`, `pinned` (string `"1"` → `true`), and `body` from the 4 parts in order.
- Reuses `extractTags(from:)` on the body — no tag logic duplicated.
- No defaulting or guessing: wrong part count = immediate error.

